From 1b388799fe5a6f10adf727d87a51a5841be9d512 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 14:30:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] avx2: real int8 GEMM over packed weights, vectorised row
 quantise

The AVX2 int8 tile widened 8 int8 to int32 and used mullo_epi32, after
gathering B one byte at a time out of the row-major copy: 8 MACs per
iteration, measured equal to scalar (4.89 vs 3.80 GFLOP/s). On the packed
[n_block][k4][16][4] layout maddubs+madd_epi16 does 32 MACs in two
instructions. The +128 offset the VNNI path uses cannot be reused
(maddubs accumulates to int16 and 255*127*2 overflows), so this uses
|a| * (b*sign(a)), which peaks at 32512 and needs no correction term:
padding is zero on both sides and |0| = 0.

quantize_rows was fully scalar and is 25.8% of device busy in a W8A8
trace, second only to the GEMM. Vectorised to be bit-identical:
round-half-away-from-zero is emulated in four instructions because
vcvtps2dq rounds half to even.

Measured on 768x768 expert shapes, one core:
  1x768x768    0.326 -> 0.075 ms   (4.3x)
  32x768x768   8.321 -> 0.548 ms   (15.2x)
  128x768x768 30.869 -> 1.960 ms   (15.7x)
  1x2048x768   0.909 -> 0.108 ms   (8.4x)

tests/isa_parity.cpp hashes raw output bytes per op so the three arms can
be compared exactly rather than within a tolerance; int8 GEMM and quantise
are bit-identical across scalar/avx2/avx512 on every shape tested,
including K%4 != 0 and N%16 != 0 edges. tests/bench_i8.cpp is the
benchmark above. docs/ZEN3-AVX2.md records the whole thing, including
which f32 ops still differ across ISAs and why that predates this change.

End to end in an engine (Kraken, 2 GiB q8_0 MoE, W8A8, forced zen3 arm):
prefill 13271 -> 3447 ms, decode 233 -> 43 ms, identical output hash.
