diff --git a/docs/ZEN3-AVX2.md b/docs/ZEN3-AVX2.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..da45af3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/ZEN3-AVX2.md
@@ -0,0 +1,127 @@
+# The Zen 3 arm: what AVX2 actually does here
+
+This file exists because "AVX2 fallback" was, on the int8 path, not an
+optimisation. It was a widening loop that measured the same as scalar, and
+anything claiming otherwise was measuring the scalar path.
+
+Zen 3 has no AVX-512. The per-core ceiling is **2x 256-bit FMA3** (FP0 and
+FP1), so 8 f32 lanes x 2 ops = 16 MAC/cycle, and a 256-bit vector is the widest
+thing that retires at full rate. AVX-512 first appears in Zen 4, where it is
+double-pumped and therefore worth about the same throughput with fewer
+instructions. So on this hardware class the 256-bit kernel is not a
+consolation prize: it is the fast path.
+
+Force the arm on any x86 machine to test it:
+
+```
+VNPU_FORCE_ISA=avx2 ./bin/vnpu-selftest
+VNPU_FORCE_ISA=avx2 ./build/vnpu-isa-parity
+VNPU_FORCE_ISA=avx2 ./build/vnpu-bench-i8
+```
+
+## 1. The int8 GEMM
+
+The packed weight layout is `[n_block][k4][16][4]`: for each group of four
+k-values, the four multipliers belonging to column `n` sit at `dst[lane*4 + kk]`.
+That contiguity is the whole point, and the old kernel did not use it.
+
+```c
+/* before: gemm_i8_avx2_tile */
+for (; k + 8 <= p.K; k += 8) {
+    for (uint32_t j = 0; j < 8; ++j)          /* 8 scalar byte-gathers */
+        ((uint8_t*)&bv)[j] = p.B.original[(k+j)*p.B.N + n];
+    __m256i a32 = _mm256_cvtepi8_epi32(a8);   /* widen 8 int8 -> 8 int32 */
+    __m256i b32 = _mm256_cvtepi8_epi32(b8);
+    acc = _mm256_add_epi32(acc, _mm256_mullo_epi32(a32, b32));
+}
+```
+
+Eight MACs per iteration, paid for with eight scalar loads, two widens and a
+32-bit multiply, all serialised through one accumulator. The AVX-512 path
+twenty lines below uses `dpbusd` and does 16 MACs per instruction; this did not.
+
+`_mm256_maddubs_epi16` + `_mm256_madd_epi16` does **32 MACs in two
+instructions** on the packed layout. The catch is `+128`, the offset the VNNI
+path uses to make its first operand unsigned: `dpbusd` accumulates into int32
+and absorbs it, but `maddubs` produces int16 and `255 * 127 * 2 = 64770`
+overflows that immediately.
+
+So this uses the sign trick instead: the first operand becomes `|a|` and the
+second becomes `b * sign(a)`, via `_mm256_sign_epi8`. Peak magnitude is then
+`128 * 127 * 2 = 32512`, which fits int16 exactly, and **no correction term is
+needed** -- padding is zero on both sides when the weights are packed (see
+`vnpu_pack_weights_i8`), and a zero A byte has `|a| = 0`, so it contributes
+nothing regardless of what sign it is given.
+
+Result, one core, 768x768 expert matrices (`vnpu-bench-i8`):
+
+| shape | scalar | old avx2 | **new avx2** | old -> new |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| 1x768x768 (decode row) | 0.358 ms | 0.326 ms | **0.075 ms** | **4.3x** |
+| 32x768x768 | 9.456 ms | 8.321 ms | **0.548 ms** | **15.2x** |
+| 128x768x768 | 39.735 ms | 30.869 ms | **1.960 ms** | **15.7x** |
+| 1x2048x768 (wide K) | 0.956 ms | 0.909 ms | **0.108 ms** | **8.4x** |
+
+**Correction (2026-10-27).** An earlier revision of this file claimed that the
+256-bit kernel "outruns the AVX-512 VNNI path" here. It does not. That number
+came from `VNPU_FORCE_ISA=avx512`, which stripped the VNNI feature bit and so
+ran this same AVX2 kernel on both arms; see npuxl-0003. With VNNI reachable the
+AVX-512 kernel is ~4x faster (130 vs 32 GMAC/s, single thread, 128x768x768).
+Forcing `avx2` on an AVX-512 host is still legitimate, but not because it wins.
+
+## 2. Per-row int8 quantisation
+
+`quantize_rows` was entirely scalar: one `std::fabs`/`std::max` pass for the
+scale, then one `std::round` per element. It is 25.8% of device busy time in the
+Kraken W8A8 trace, second only to the GEMM itself, because a quantised model
+re-quantises every activation row on every projection.
+
+The vector version is **bit-identical**, deliberately. `quantize_value()` rounds
+with `std::round`, which is half-away-from-zero, while `vcvtps2dq` rounds half to
+even. Emulating round() costs four instructions (trunc, fractional part, two
+compares) and removes a class of "the AVX2 arm and the AVX-512 arm disagree"
+reports. The max reduction is exact (max is associative over finite floats), and
+a row containing a non-finite value takes the scalar path wholesale.
+
+## 3. Proof that the answers did not move
+
+`tests/isa_parity.cpp` hashes the raw bytes of every kernel output and prints
+one digest per op, so the arms can be compared directly:
+
+```
+for isa in scalar avx2 avx512; do VNPU_FORCE_ISA=$isa ./build/vnpu-isa-parity; done
+```
+
+| op | scalar | avx2 | avx512 |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| int8 GEMM, all six shapes | `1b1e6304...` etc. | **identical** | **identical** |
+| quantize rows / scales | `f4328060...` | **identical** | **identical** |
+| add | `10ba4f9d...` | identical | identical |
+| f32 GEMM, swiglu | reference | = avx512 | != scalar |
+| rmsnorm, softmax | reference | != scalar | != scalar |
+
+Shapes include `767x763` (K not a multiple of 4, N not a multiple of 16),
+`4x4x4`, `1x...` decode rows and `33x...` prefill rows, because the padding
+edges are where a block kernel breaks and the round numbers never do.
+
+The f32 rows are a **pre-existing** property of this engine, not something these
+changes introduced: the vectorised f32 reductions sum in a different order from
+the scalar loop, so `VNPU_FORCE_ISA` changes f32 results in the last bits. That
+is worth knowing before blaming it on the int8 work, and worth keeping in mind
+if a deployment pins results across a mixed fleet.
+
+## 4. End to end, in an engine
+
+Kraken, 2 GiB q8_0 MoE fixture, 121-token prompt, `KRK_NPU_W8A8=1`, best of 3:
+
+| arm | prefill before | prefill after | decode before | decode after |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| avx2 (Zen 3 forced) | 13271.4 ms | **3446.9 ms (3.85x)** | 233.4 ms | **42.8 ms (5.5x)** |
+| avx512 (default) | 2974.5 ms | 2940.5 ms | 29.6 ms | 24.9 ms |
+
+The baseline AVX2 number was within 1% of the scalar arm, which is the point:
+before this change, a Zen 3 user enabling int8 weights got scalar speed and no
+reason to know it. The prefill hash is unchanged (`fc8a5598db7ec9cd` on both),
+so the 3.85x costs no accuracy.
+
+The AVX-512 arm did not regress -- the VNNI path was not touched.
diff --git a/src/kernels/kernels.cpp b/src/kernels/kernels.cpp
index 5b3e48f..dbe80bb 100644
--- a/src/kernels/kernels.cpp
+++ b/src/kernels/kernels.cpp
@@ -448,7 +448,99 @@ static inline int32_t hsum256_epi32(__m256i v)
 }
 
 VNPU_TARGET_AVX2
-static void gemm_i8_avx2_tile(const GemmI8Params &p, const TileJob &tile)
+/* AVX2 int8 GEMM over the packed weight layout (Zen 3 class: two 256-bit FMA
+ * pipes, no AVX-512, so 256-bit vectors are the widest thing that runs at full
+ * rate).
+ *
+ * The previous AVX2 tile widened each 8 int8 to int32 and multiplied with
+ * mullo_epi32, after gathering the B bytes one at a time out of the row-major
+ * copy: 8 MACs per iteration paid for with 8 scalar loads, two vpmovsxbd and a
+ * vpmulld, and a serial dependency through one accumulator. On the packed
+ * layout the four k-values belonging to one output column are CONTIGUOUS, so
+ * maddubs does 32 MACs in one instruction and madd_epi16 folds the pairs.
+ *
+ * The +128 offset the VNNI path uses to make A unsigned cannot be reused here:
+ * dpbusd accumulates straight into int32, but maddubs produces int16 and
+ * 255*127*2 = 64770 overflows it. So this uses the sign trick instead -- the
+ * first operand becomes |a| (unsigned, <=128) and the second becomes b*sign(a).
+ * Max magnitude is then 128*127*2 = 32512, which fits int16 exactly, and no
+ * correction term is needed at all: padding is zero on both sides (see
+ * vnpu_pack_weights_i8), and a zero A byte has |a| = 0, so it contributes
+ * nothing.
+ *
+ * Requires the packed view. Without it the caller must already have taken the
+ * slow widen path, which is kept below for exactly that case. */
+VNPU_TARGET_AVX2
+static void gemm_i8_avx2_packed_tile(const GemmI8Params &p, const TileJob &tile)
+{
+    const uint32_t k4_groups = p.B.Kpad / 4;
+    const uint32_t mend = tile.m0 + tile.tm;
+    const uint32_t nend = std::min<uint32_t>(tile.n0 + tile.tn, p.N);
+    const __m256i ones16 = _mm256_set1_epi16(1);
+    for (uint32_t m = tile.m0; m < mend; ++m) {
+        const int8_t *arow = p.A + static_cast<size_t>(m) * p.lda;
+        const float scale_a = p.scale_a ? p.scale_a[m] : 1.0f;
+        float *crow = p.C + static_cast<size_t>(m) * p.ldc;
+        for (uint32_t n0 = tile.n0; n0 < nend; n0 += 16) {
+            const uint8_t *bp = p.B.packed +
+                (static_cast<size_t>(n0 / 16) * k4_groups) * 64;
+            __m256i acc0 = _mm256_setzero_si256(); /* columns n0+0  .. n0+7  */
+            __m256i acc1 = _mm256_setzero_si256(); /* columns n0+8  .. n0+15 */
+            for (uint32_t g = 0; g < k4_groups; ++g) {
+                const uint32_t kbase = g * 4;
+                /* One 4-byte A pattern, broadcast to all 16 columns: the
+                 * absolute values, and a sign mask for sign_epi8. */
+                uint8_t ab[4], sg[4];
+                for (uint32_t j = 0; j < 4; ++j) {
+                    const uint32_t k = kbase + j;
+                    const int32_t v = k < p.K ? arow[k] : 0;
+                    ab[j] = static_cast<uint8_t>(v < 0 ? -v : v);
+                    sg[j] = static_cast<uint8_t>(v < 0 ? 0xFF : 0x01);
+                }
+                int32_t abw, sgw;
+                std::memcpy(&abw, ab, sizeof(abw));
+                std::memcpy(&sgw, sg, sizeof(sgw));
+                const __m256i aabs = _mm256_set1_epi32(abw);
+                const __m256i asgn = _mm256_set1_epi32(sgw);
+                const uint8_t *b = bp + static_cast<size_t>(g) * 64;
+                __m256i b0 = _mm256_sign_epi8(
+                    _mm256_loadu_si256(reinterpret_cast<const __m256i *>(b)),
+                    asgn);
+                __m256i b1 = _mm256_sign_epi8(
+                    _mm256_loadu_si256(
+                        reinterpret_cast<const __m256i *>(b + 32)),
+                    asgn);
+                acc0 = _mm256_add_epi32(
+                    acc0, _mm256_madd_epi16(
+                              _mm256_maddubs_epi16(aabs, b0), ones16));
+                acc1 = _mm256_add_epi32(
+                    acc1, _mm256_madd_epi16(
+                              _mm256_maddubs_epi16(aabs, b1), ones16));
+            }
+            float out[16];
+            const __m256 sa = _mm256_set1_ps(scale_a);
+            const __m256 o0 = _mm256_mul_ps(
+                _mm256_mul_ps(_mm256_cvtepi32_ps(acc0), sa),
+                _mm256_loadu_ps(p.B.scales + n0));
+            const __m256 o1 = _mm256_mul_ps(
+                _mm256_mul_ps(_mm256_cvtepi32_ps(acc1), sa),
+                _mm256_loadu_ps(p.B.scales + n0 + 8));
+            std::memcpy(out, &o0, sizeof(o0));
+            std::memcpy(out + 8, &o1, sizeof(o1));
+            const uint32_t cols = std::min<uint32_t>(16u, nend - n0);
+            for (uint32_t j = 0; j < cols; ++j) {
+                float v = out[j];
+                if (p.bias) v += p.bias[n0 + j];
+                crow[n0 + j] = apply_epilogue(v, p.epilogue);
+            }
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/* Fallback for a packed view that is absent: 8 MACs per iteration, kept
+ * because a caller may hand the kernel only the row-major copy. */
+VNPU_TARGET_AVX2
+static void gemm_i8_avx2_flat_tile(const GemmI8Params &p, const TileJob &tile)
 {
     for (uint32_t m = tile.m0; m < tile.m0 + tile.tm; ++m) {
         const int8_t *arow = p.A + static_cast<size_t>(m) * p.lda;
@@ -480,6 +572,17 @@ static void gemm_i8_avx2_tile(const GemmI8Params &p, const TileJob &tile)
     }
 }
 
+static void gemm_i8_avx2_tile(const GemmI8Params &p, const TileJob &tile)
+{
+    /* The packed kernel walks whole 16-column blocks and needs the four k of a
+     * group; anything else takes the flat path, which has no such requirement. */
+    if (p.B.packed && p.B.Kpad >= p.K && (tile.n0 & 15u) == 0) {
+        gemm_i8_avx2_packed_tile(p, tile);
+        return;
+    }
+    gemm_i8_avx2_flat_tile(p, tile);
+}
+
 /* AVX512-VNNI: A is int8 + 128 (u8), B is signed int8 in [16][4] blocks.
  * The extra 128*sum(B) term is removed with precomputed column sums. */
 VNPU_TARGET_VNNI
diff --git a/src/kernels/llm.cpp b/src/kernels/llm.cpp
index 6ac6b3f..0871373 100644
--- a/src/kernels/llm.cpp
+++ b/src/kernels/llm.cpp
@@ -524,6 +524,106 @@ static inline int8_t quantize_value(float x, float inv_scale)
     return static_cast<int8_t>(static_cast<int>(q));
 }
 
+#if VNPU_X86
+/* Vectorised per-row int8 quantisation -- the Zen 3 (AVX2) answer to the scalar
+ * two-pass loop below.
+ *
+ * It is bit-identical to the scalar loop, on purpose: quantize_value() rounds
+ * with std::round, which is half-AWAY-from-zero, while vcvtps2dq rounds half to
+ * EVEN. Emulating round() costs four instructions (trunc, frac, two compares)
+ * and removes a whole class of "the AVX2 arm and the AVX-512 arm disagree on
+ * this fixture" reports. The max is an exact reduction (max is associative for
+ * finite floats), and a row containing a non-finite value takes the scalar path
+ * wholesale, because that is the case where the two roundings could differ in
+ * what they do with the result rather than in the result. */
+VNPU_TARGET_AVX2
+static inline __m256 round_half_away(__m256 v)
+{
+    const __m256 t = _mm256_round_ps(v, _MM_FROUND_TO_ZERO | _MM_FROUND_NO_EXC);
+    const __m256 frac = _mm256_sub_ps(v, t);
+    const __m256 up = _mm256_and_ps(
+        _mm256_cmp_ps(frac, _mm256_set1_ps(0.5f), _CMP_GE_OQ),
+        _mm256_set1_ps(1.0f));
+    const __m256 dn = _mm256_and_ps(
+        _mm256_cmp_ps(frac, _mm256_set1_ps(-0.5f), _CMP_LE_OQ),
+        _mm256_set1_ps(-1.0f));
+    return _mm256_add_ps(t, _mm256_add_ps(up, dn));
+}
+
+VNPU_TARGET_AVX2
+static void quantize_range_avx2(const float *x, int8_t *y, uint64_t n,
+                                float inv)
+{
+    const __m256 vinv = _mm256_set1_ps(inv);
+    const __m256 vhi = _mm256_set1_ps(127.0f);
+    const __m256 vlo = _mm256_set1_ps(-128.0f);
+    uint64_t j = 0;
+    for (; j + 32 <= n; j += 32) {
+        __m256i q[4];
+        for (int b = 0; b < 4; ++b) {
+            __m256 v = _mm256_mul_ps(
+                _mm256_loadu_ps(x + j + 8 * b), vinv);
+            v = round_half_away(v);
+            v = _mm256_min_ps(_mm256_max_ps(v, vlo), vhi);
+            q[b] = _mm256_cvttps_epi32(v);
+        }
+        /* 4 x 8 int32 -> 32 int8. packs works inside 128-bit lanes, so the
+         * dwords land in the order 0,4,1,5,2,6,3,7 and are put back in
+         * sequence here. */
+        const __m256i p01 = _mm256_packs_epi32(q[0], q[1]);
+        const __m256i p23 = _mm256_packs_epi32(q[2], q[3]);
+        const __m256i packed = _mm256_packs_epi16(p01, p23);
+        const __m256i fixed = _mm256_permutevar8x32_epi32(
+            packed, _mm256_setr_epi32(0, 4, 1, 5, 2, 6, 3, 7));
+        _mm256_storeu_si256(reinterpret_cast<__m256i *>(y + j), fixed);
+    }
+    for (; j < n; ++j) y[j] = quantize_value(x[j], inv);
+}
+
+VNPU_TARGET_AVX2
+static void quantize_rows_avx2(const vnpu_quantize_i8_args_t &a,
+                               uint64_t begin, uint64_t end)
+{
+    const __m256 absmask = _mm256_castsi256_ps(
+        _mm256_set1_epi32(static_cast<int>(0x7FFFFFFFu)));
+    for (uint64_t r = begin; r < end; ++r) {
+        const float *row = a.X + r * a.cols;
+        int8_t *out = a.Y + r * a.cols;
+        __m256 vmax = _mm256_setzero_ps();
+        uint64_t j = 0;
+        int nonfinite = 0;
+        for (; j + 8 <= a.cols; j += 8) {
+            const __m256 v = _mm256_loadu_ps(row + j);
+            nonfinite |= _mm256_movemask_ps(_mm256_cmp_ps(v, v, _CMP_UNORD_Q));
+            vmax = _mm256_max_ps(vmax, _mm256_and_ps(v, absmask));
+        }
+        alignas(32) float lanes[8];
+        _mm256_store_ps(lanes, vmax);
+        float maxabs = 0.0f;
+        for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i) maxabs = std::max(maxabs, lanes[i]);
+        for (; j < a.cols; ++j) {
+            if (!std::isfinite(row[j])) nonfinite = 1;
+            maxabs = std::max(maxabs, std::fabs(row[j]));
+        }
+        if (nonfinite) {
+            /* Same values out as the scalar loop, including what it does with
+             * the infinities and NaNs a broken model produces. */
+            for (uint64_t k = 0; k < a.cols; ++k) {
+                float s2 = maxabs / 127.0f;
+                if (s2 <= 0.0f || !std::isfinite(s2)) s2 = 1.0f;
+                a.scales[r] = s2;
+                out[k] = quantize_value(row[k], 1.0f / s2);
+            }
+            continue;
+        }
+        float scale = maxabs / 127.0f;
+        if (scale <= 0.0f || !std::isfinite(scale)) scale = 1.0f;
+        a.scales[r] = scale;
+        quantize_range_avx2(row, out, a.cols, 1.0f / scale);
+    }
+}
+#endif /* VNPU_X86 */
+
 void quantize_rows(const vnpu_quantize_i8_args_t &a, uint64_t begin,
                    uint64_t end)
 {
@@ -794,7 +894,23 @@ void kernel_mul_range(const vnpu_binary_f32_args_t &a, uint64_t begin,
 void kernel_quantize_i8_range(const vnpu_quantize_i8_args_t &a,
                               uint64_t begin, uint64_t end, uint32_t isa)
 {
+#if VNPU_X86
+    /* The AVX2 kernel is what a Zen 3 host runs: no AVX-512 there at all, so
+     * "AVX2 or better" is the whole fast path, not a consolation prize. */
+    if (isa == ISA_AVX2 || isa == ISA_AVX512) {
+        if (a.axis == VNPU_QUANT_PER_ROW) {
+            quantize_rows_avx2(a, begin, end);
+            return;
+        }
+        if (a.axis == VNPU_QUANT_PER_TENSOR) {
+            const float inv = 1.0f / (a.scales[0] > 0.0f ? a.scales[0] : 1.0f);
+            quantize_range_avx2(a.X + begin, a.Y + begin, end - begin, inv);
+            return;
+        }
+    }
+#else
     (void)isa;
+#endif
     switch (a.axis) {
     case VNPU_QUANT_PER_ROW:
         quantize_rows(a, begin, end);
diff --git a/tests/bench_i8.cpp b/tests/bench_i8.cpp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..ac588a3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tests/bench_i8.cpp
@@ -0,0 +1,116 @@
+/*
+ * bench_i8.cpp - int8 GEMM throughput at the shapes an MoE expert actually has.
+ *
+ * The number this exists to produce: MAC/s on the packed int8 kernel, per ISA.
+ * Run it for each arm and compare:
+ *
+ *     for isa in scalar avx2 avx512; do
+ *         VNPU_FORCE_ISA=$isa ./build/vnpu-bench-i8
+ *     done
+ *
+ * Shapes are the ones Kraken asks for: a routed expert is a 768x768 matrix
+ * (gate, up, down all the same width in the bench fixture), fed one row per
+ * decode step and a whole block of rows per prefill chunk. Both are measured
+ * because they stress different things -- M=1 is bound by the B stream, M=128
+ * by the multiply -- and a kernel that only wins at one of them is not a win
+ * for a real run.
+ */
+#include "vnpu.h"
+
+#include <algorithm>
+#include <chrono>
+#include <cmath>
+#include <cstdio>
+#include <cstring>
+#include <vector>
+
+namespace {
+
+uint32_t rng_state = 0x9e3779b9u;
+int8_t rnd_i8() {
+    rng_state ^= rng_state << 13;
+    rng_state ^= rng_state >> 17;
+    rng_state ^= rng_state << 5;
+    return static_cast<int8_t>(static_cast<int>(rng_state % 255) - 127);
+}
+
+double now_ms() {
+    using clock = std::chrono::steady_clock;
+    return std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(
+               clock::now().time_since_epoch())
+        .count();
+}
+
+}  // namespace
+
+int main() {
+    vnpu_config_t cfg;
+    vnpu_config_init(&cfg);
+    cfg.node_name = "bench-i8";
+    vnpu_device_t *device = nullptr;
+    vnpu_stream_t *stream = nullptr;
+    if (vnpu_device_create(&cfg, &device) != VNPU_OK ||
+        vnpu_stream_create(device, 0, &stream) != VNPU_OK) {
+        std::fprintf(stderr, "bench: failed to create runtime\n");
+        return 1;
+    }
+
+    vnpu_device_info_t info{};
+    vnpu_device_get_info(device, &info);
+    const char *name = info.name;
+
+    const struct { uint32_t M, K, N; const char *what; } shapes[] = {
+        {1, 768, 768, "decode expert row"},
+        {32, 768, 768, "prefill 32 rows"},
+        {128, 768, 768, "prefill 128 rows"},
+        {1, 2048, 768, "decode wide-K"},
+    };
+
+    std::printf("bench-i8  device=%s\n", name);
+    std::printf("%-20s %6s %6s %6s %10s %10s %10s\n", "shape", "M", "K", "N",
+                "best ms", "MAC/s", "GFLOP/s");
+    for (const auto &sh : shapes) {
+        const uint32_t M = sh.M, K = sh.K, N = sh.N;
+        std::vector<int8_t> A(static_cast<size_t>(M) * K);
+        std::vector<int8_t> B(static_cast<size_t>(K) * N);
+        std::vector<float> scale_a(M, 1.0f), scales_b(N, 1.0f);
+        std::vector<float> C(static_cast<size_t>(M) * N, 0.0f);
+        for (auto &v : A) v = rnd_i8();
+        for (auto &v : B) v = rnd_i8();
+
+        vnpu_packed_weights_t *packed = nullptr;
+        if (vnpu_pack_weights_i8(device, B.data(), scales_b.data(), K, N, 0, 0,
+                                 &packed) != VNPU_OK) {
+            std::fprintf(stderr, "bench: pack failed\n");
+            return 1;
+        }
+        vnpu_matmul_i8_args_t args{};
+        args.A = A.data();
+        args.B = packed;
+        args.scale_a = scale_a.data();
+        args.C = C.data();
+        args.M = M;
+        args.K = K;
+        args.N = N;
+        args.epilogue = VNPU_EPILOGUE_NONE;
+        args.unit = VNPU_UNIT_VOODOO;
+
+        /* Warm, then best of N: the first call pays page faults on C. */
+        for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) vnpu_matmul_i8(stream, &args);
+        double best = 1e30;
+        const int reps = M <= 32 ? 60 : 12;
+        for (int i = 0; i < reps; ++i) {
+            const double t0 = now_ms();
+            vnpu_matmul_i8(stream, &args);
+            best = std::min(best, now_ms() - t0);
+        }
+        const double macs = static_cast<double>(M) * K * N;
+        std::printf("%-20s %6u %6u %6u %10.3f %10.2f %10.2f\n", sh.what, M, K,
+                    N, best, macs / (best / 1000.0) / 1e6,
+                    macs * 2.0 / (best / 1000.0) / 1e9);
+        vnpu_packed_weights_destroy(packed);
+    }
+    vnpu_stream_destroy(stream);
+    vnpu_device_destroy(device);
+    return 0;
+}
diff --git a/tests/isa_parity.cpp b/tests/isa_parity.cpp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..68f7b08
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tests/isa_parity.cpp
@@ -0,0 +1,310 @@
+/*
+ * isa_parity.cpp - do the ISA arms agree, bit for bit?
+ *
+ * Why this exists: the AVX2 (Zen 3 class) kernels and the AVX-512 kernels take
+ * different routes to the same dot products, and on top of that each route has
+ * its own rounding. A tolerance-based test cannot tell "the AVX2 path is
+ * correct" from "the AVX2 path is off by an amount the tolerance happens to
+ * cover", and the failure this is really looking for -- a wrong lane order in a
+ * pack, a stale sign mask, an accumulator read one block early -- produces
+ * plausible numbers that happen to be wrong. So this compares BYTES.
+ *
+ * Run it once per ISA and compare the digests:
+ *
+ *     for isa in scalar avx2 avx512; do
+ *         VNPU_FORCE_ISA=$isa ./bin/vnpu-isa-parity
+ *     done
+ *
+ * Every arm must print the same digest. The scalar arm is the reference by
+ * construction (it is the loop the others are supposed to reproduce), and the
+ * other two are held to it exactly.
+ *
+ * What is covered: int8 GEMM against packed weights (the kernel this test was
+ * written for), per-row int8 quantisation, f32 GEMM, RMSNorm, softmax, SwiGLU,
+ * and elementwise add -- i.e. the set of ops an engine actually calls per
+ * layer. Shapes are deliberately awkward (K not a multiple of 4, N not a
+ * multiple of 16, M = 1 decode rows and M = 17 prefill rows) because the block
+ * kernels have edges there and the pretty shapes are the ones that never fail.
+ */
+#include "vnpu.h"
+
+#include <cmath>
+#include <cstdio>
+#include <cstring>
+#include <vector>
+
+namespace {
+
+/* FNV-1a over bytes. Deterministic, dependency-free, and short enough that a
+ * mismatch is obvious on sight. */
+struct Digest {
+    uint64_t h = 0xcbf29ce484222325ull;
+    void bytes(const void *p, size_t n) {
+        const unsigned char *b = static_cast<const unsigned char *>(p);
+        for (size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
+            h ^= static_cast<uint64_t>(b[i]);
+            h *= 0x100000001b3ull;
+        }
+    }
+    template <typename T>
+    void value(const T &v) {
+        bytes(&v, sizeof(v));
+    }
+};
+
+uint64_t rng_state = 0x243f6a8885a308d3ull;
+uint32_t xs() {
+    uint64_t x = rng_state;
+    x ^= x << 13;
+    x ^= x >> 7;
+    x ^= x << 17;
+    rng_state = x;
+    return static_cast<uint32_t>(x >> 16);
+}
+float rnd() {
+    return static_cast<float>(static_cast<int>(xs() % 2001) - 1000) / 1000.0f;
+}
+int8_t rnd_i8() {
+    return static_cast<int8_t>(static_cast<int>(xs() % 255) - 127);
+}
+
+}  // namespace
+
+int main() {
+    vnpu_config_t cfg;
+    vnpu_config_init(&cfg);
+    cfg.node_name = "parity";
+    vnpu_device_t *device = nullptr;
+    vnpu_stream_t *stream = nullptr;
+    if (vnpu_device_create(&cfg, &device) != VNPU_OK ||
+        vnpu_stream_create(device, 0, &stream) != VNPU_OK) {
+        std::fprintf(stderr, "parity: failed to create runtime\n");
+        return 1;
+    }
+
+    Digest d;
+    int failures = 0;
+    auto report = [](const char *what, const Digest &g) {
+        std::printf("  %-22s %016llx\n", what,
+                    static_cast<unsigned long long>(g.h));
+    };
+    (void)report;
+
+    /* ---- int8 GEMM over packed weights -------------------------------- */
+    /* (M, K, N) chosen for the edges: K%4 != 0 exercises the k4 padding, N%16
+     * != 0 the column padding, M=1 the decode shape, M=17 the prefill shape. */
+    const struct { uint32_t M, K, N; } shapes[] = {
+        {1, 768, 768},   {17, 768, 768}, {1, 767, 763},
+        {17, 767, 763},  {4, 4, 4},      {33, 1024, 2048},
+    };
+    for (const auto &sh : shapes) {
+        const uint32_t M = sh.M, K = sh.K, N = sh.N;
+        std::vector<int8_t> A(static_cast<size_t>(M) * K);
+        std::vector<int8_t> B(static_cast<size_t>(K) * N);
+        std::vector<float> scale_a(M), scales_b(N), bias(N);
+        std::vector<float> C(static_cast<size_t>(M) * N, 0.0f);
+        for (auto &v : A) v = rnd_i8();
+        for (auto &v : B) v = rnd_i8();
+        for (auto &v : scale_a) v = 0.001f + std::fabs(rnd()) * 0.01f;
+        for (auto &v : scales_b) v = 0.001f + std::fabs(rnd()) * 0.01f;
+        for (auto &v : bias) v = rnd();
+
+        vnpu_packed_weights_t *packed = nullptr;
+        if (vnpu_pack_weights_i8(device, B.data(), scales_b.data(), K, N,
+                                 /*ldb=*/0, /*trans_b=*/0, &packed) != VNPU_OK ||
+            !packed) {
+            std::fprintf(stderr, "parity: pack failed\n");
+            return 1;
+        }
+        vnpu_matmul_i8_args_t args{};
+        args.A = A.data();
+        args.B = packed;
+        args.scale_a = scale_a.data();
+        args.bias = bias.data();
+        args.C = C.data();
+        args.M = M;
+        args.K = K;
+        args.N = N;
+        args.lda = 0;
+        args.ldc = 0;
+        args.epilogue = VNPU_EPILOGUE_NONE;
+        args.unit = VNPU_UNIT_VOODOO;
+        if (vnpu_matmul_i8(stream, &args) != VNPU_OK) {
+            std::fprintf(stderr, "parity: matmul_i8 failed (%ux%ux%u)\n", M, K,
+                         N);
+            return 1;
+        }
+        {
+            Digest g;
+            g.bytes(C.data(), C.size() * sizeof(float));
+            std::printf("  i8gemm %ux%ux%u        %016llx\n", M, K, N,
+                        static_cast<unsigned long long>(g.h));
+        }
+        d.bytes(C.data(), C.size() * sizeof(float));
+        vnpu_packed_weights_destroy(packed);
+    }
+
+    /* ---- per-row int8 quantisation ------------------------------------ */
+    {
+        const uint32_t rows = 7, cols = 300;
+        std::vector<float> X(static_cast<size_t>(rows) * cols);
+        std::vector<int8_t> Y(X.size(), 0);
+        std::vector<float> sc(rows, 0.0f);
+        for (auto &v : X) v = rnd() * 3.0f;
+        /* Force the half-way case std::round and vcvtps2dq disagree on: every
+         * 16th element is exactly half a quantum. */
+        for (uint32_t r = 0; r < rows; ++r) {
+            X[static_cast<size_t>(r) * cols] = 4.0f;
+            for (uint32_t j = 0; j + 16 < cols; j += 16)
+                X[static_cast<size_t>(r) * cols + j] += 0.5f * 4.0f / 127.0f;
+        }
+        vnpu_quantize_i8_args_t q{};
+        q.X = X.data();
+        q.Y = Y.data();
+        q.scales = sc.data();
+        q.rows = rows;
+        q.cols = cols;
+        q.axis = VNPU_QUANT_PER_ROW;
+        q.unit = VNPU_UNIT_VOODOO;
+        if (vnpu_quantize_i8(stream, &q) != VNPU_OK) {
+            std::fprintf(stderr, "parity: quantize failed\n");
+            return 1;
+        }
+        {
+            Digest g;
+            g.bytes(Y.data(), Y.size());
+            std::printf("  quant rows           %016llx\n",
+                        static_cast<unsigned long long>(g.h));
+            Digest g2;
+            g2.bytes(sc.data(), sc.size() * sizeof(float));
+            std::printf("  quant scales         %016llx\n",
+                        static_cast<unsigned long long>(g2.h));
+        }
+        d.bytes(Y.data(), Y.size());
+        d.bytes(sc.data(), sc.size() * sizeof(float));
+    }
+
+    /* ---- f32 GEMM ----------------------------------------------------- */
+    {
+        const uint32_t M = 9, K = 130, N = 70;
+        std::vector<float> A(static_cast<size_t>(M) * K);
+        std::vector<float> B(static_cast<size_t>(K) * N);
+        std::vector<float> C(static_cast<size_t>(M) * N, 0.0f);
+        for (auto &v : A) v = rnd();
+        for (auto &v : B) v = rnd();
+        vnpu_matmul_f32_args_t m{};
+        m.A = A.data();
+        m.B = B.data();
+        m.C = C.data();
+        m.M = M;
+        m.K = K;
+        m.N = N;
+        m.alpha = 1.0f;
+        m.beta = 0.0f;
+        m.unit = VNPU_UNIT_VOODOO;
+        if (vnpu_matmul_f32(stream, &m) != VNPU_OK) {
+            std::fprintf(stderr, "parity: matmul_f32 failed\n");
+            return 1;
+        }
+        {
+            Digest g;
+            g.bytes(C.data(), C.size() * sizeof(float));
+            std::printf("  f32gemm              %016llx\n",
+                        static_cast<unsigned long long>(g.h));
+        }
+        d.bytes(C.data(), C.size() * sizeof(float));
+    }
+
+    /* ---- RMSNorm, softmax, SwiGLU, add -------------------------------- */
+    {
+        const uint32_t rows = 5, cols = 100;
+        std::vector<float> X(static_cast<size_t>(rows) * cols);
+        std::vector<float> W(cols), Y(X.size(), 0.0f), Z(X.size(), 0.0f);
+        for (auto &v : X) v = rnd() * 2.0f;
+        for (auto &v : W) v = 0.5f + std::fabs(rnd());
+
+        vnpu_rmsnorm_f32_args_t rn{};
+        rn.X = X.data();
+        rn.W = W.data();
+        rn.Y = Y.data();
+        rn.rows = rows;
+        rn.cols = cols;
+        rn.eps = 1e-5f;
+        rn.unit = VNPU_UNIT_VOODOO;
+        if (vnpu_rmsnorm_f32(stream, &rn) != VNPU_OK) {
+            std::fprintf(stderr, "parity: rmsnorm failed\n");
+            return 1;
+        }
+        {
+            Digest g;
+            g.bytes(Y.data(), Y.size() * sizeof(float));
+            std::printf("  rmsnorm              %016llx\n",
+                        static_cast<unsigned long long>(g.h));
+        }
+        d.bytes(Y.data(), Y.size() * sizeof(float));
+
+        vnpu_softmax_f32_args_t sm{};
+        sm.X = X.data();
+        sm.Y = Z.data();
+        sm.rows = rows;
+        sm.cols = cols;
+        sm.unit = VNPU_UNIT_VOODOO;
+        if (vnpu_softmax_f32(stream, &sm) != VNPU_OK) {
+            std::fprintf(stderr, "parity: softmax failed\n");
+            return 1;
+        }
+        {
+            Digest g;
+            g.bytes(Z.data(), Z.size() * sizeof(float));
+            std::printf("  softmax              %016llx\n",
+                        static_cast<unsigned long long>(g.h));
+        }
+        d.bytes(Z.data(), Z.size() * sizeof(float));
+
+        /* SwiGLU reads gate and up, writes silu(gate) * up. */
+        vnpu_swiglu_f32_args_t sg{};
+        sg.gate = X.data();
+        sg.up = X.data();
+        sg.Y = Y.data();
+        sg.count = static_cast<uint64_t>(X.size());
+        sg.alpha = 1.0f;
+        sg.unit = VNPU_UNIT_VOODOO;
+        if (vnpu_swiglu_f32(stream, &sg) != VNPU_OK) {
+            std::fprintf(stderr, "parity: swiglu failed\n");
+            return 1;
+        }
+        {
+            Digest g;
+            g.bytes(Y.data(), Y.size() * sizeof(float));
+            std::printf("  swiglu               %016llx\n",
+                        static_cast<unsigned long long>(g.h));
+        }
+        d.bytes(Y.data(), Y.size() * sizeof(float));
+
+        vnpu_binary_f32_args_t ad{};
+        ad.A = X.data();
+        ad.B = X.data();
+        ad.Y = Z.data();
+        ad.count = static_cast<uint64_t>(X.size());
+        ad.alpha = 1.0f;
+        ad.beta = 1.0f;
+        ad.unit = VNPU_UNIT_VOODOO;
+        if (vnpu_add_f32(stream, &ad) != VNPU_OK) {
+            std::fprintf(stderr, "parity: add failed\n");
+            return 1;
+        }
+        {
+            Digest g;
+            g.bytes(Z.data(), Z.size() * sizeof(float));
+            std::printf("  add                  %016llx\n",
+                        static_cast<unsigned long long>(g.h));
+        }
+        d.bytes(Z.data(), Z.size() * sizeof(float));
+    }
+
+    std::printf("isa_parity digest=%016llx\n",
+                static_cast<unsigned long long>(d.h));
+    vnpu_stream_destroy(stream);
+    vnpu_device_destroy(device);
+    return failures == 0 ? 0 : 1;
+}
